auth-server is Phase 1 of a centralized login service (port 62000) every other Shattered Archive app will eventually validate bearer tokens against — it does nothing by itself except manage accounts and issue/verify per-service API keys mapped to one master identity. Signup is open and username-based, gated by a 3-question anti-bot challenge drawn from an operator-hand-edited plain-JSON pool (dsl-questions.json — deliberately NOT encrypted, unlike every other store, because an operator can't hand-edit ciphertext); it issues a system-generated one-time password that forces a mandatory change on first login. Sessions are just short-TTL key records (kind:'session'), not a separate mechanism — one verify() path in key-store.ts covers both browser sessions and API keys. The central invariant is the epoch counter: every account has one, every key/session records the epoch it was minted at, and a mismatch invalidates it instantly — so a password change or an explicit rotate-master invalidates every previously issued credential with no need to touch each record individually, at the cost of both routes needing to mint a FRESH session before responding (or the caller would appear logged out right after the action that was meant to keep them in). Every JSON store except the questions pool is encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM, crypto-primitives.ts) under a key that is never stored alongside the data it protects — loadDataKey() prefers a mounted file (self-generated on first boot, persists across container recreation via the auth-server-secrets Docker volume) over the DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY env var (local dev/test only). /api/introspect is server-to-server only, gated by a per-service Ed25519 signed assertion (service-key-store.ts) that supports multiple concurrently-valid keys per service so rotation never causes an outage — never reachable via a browser session cookie. Phase 2 (apps/auth-client, complete) added the browser UI and one real consumer — mud-builder-server's GET /api/auth/introspect-check — actually calling /api/introspect; this service was already fully deployable on its own (deploy/auth-server.Dockerfile + both docker-compose files + the auth.shatteredarchive.dev nginx block, later split between auth-server and auth-client) before that UI existed. Phase 15 added login-lockout.ts: an in-memory, fail2ban-style escalating soft lockout on POST /api/auth/login, tracked independently by normalized username AND by source IP (either being locked blocks the attempt, even with the correct password) — a fixed number of free failures, then each further one doubles the lockout duration up to a cap, always eventually expiring (never a permanent ban, no OS-level enforcement — pure in-app counting, same "resets on restart" tradeoff ChallengeThrottle already makes).
